{"id":"neet-2026-q107","pn":107,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Bulliform cells","q":"The main function of bulliform cells in grasses is :","o":["to make the leaf impermeable to fungal spores.","to perform photosynthesis.","to minimize water loss during water stress.","to transport water."],"c":2,"s":"Bulliform cells are large, empty, colourless cells.\nIn water-scarce conditions they lose water and become flaccid.\nThen the leaf curls inwards, which reduces the exposed surface area and so minimises water loss."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q109","pn":109,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Tissues and cell types","q":"Match List-I with List-II :\nList-I — List-II\nA. Conjunctive tissue — I. Specialised cells in the vicinity of guard cells\nB. Casparian strips — II. Endodermal cells rich in starch\nC. Subsidiary cells — III. Tissue between xylem and phloem\nD. Starch sheath — IV. Endodermal cells with suberin deposition","o":["A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"c":2,"s":"A. Conjunctive tissue is the tissue between xylem and phloem.\nB. Casparian strips are found in endodermal cells; they are suberin depositions in the cell wall.\nC. Subsidiary cells are specialised cells in the vicinity of guard cells.\nD. Starch sheath is another name for endodermal cells rich in starch."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q118","pn":118,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Monocot stem","q":"Find the statement that is NOT correct with regard to the structure of monocot stem.","o":["Hypodermis is parenchymatous.","Vascular bundles are scattered.","Vascular bundles are conjoint and closed.","Phloem parenchyma is absent."],"c":0,"s":"In monocot stem, the hypodermis is sclerenchymatous, not parenchymatous."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q105","pn":105,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Bulliform cells in monocot leaf","q":"Builiform cells are responsible for","o":["Increased photosynthesis in monocots","Providing large spaces for storage of sugar","Inward curling of leaves in monocots","Protecting the plant from salt stress"],"c":2,"s":"In grasses, some adaxial epidermal cells along the veins become large, empty, colourless cells called bulliform cells.\nWhen they are turgid (full of water), the leaf surface is exposed.\nWhen they are flaccid due to water stress, they make the leaf curl inwards, which reduces water loss."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q132","pn":132,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Permanent tissues","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Parenchyma is living but collenchyma is dead tissue\nStatement II: Gymnosperms lack xylem vessels but presence of xylem vessels is the characteristic of angiosperms.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"c":1,"s":"Collenchyma is also a living tissue, so Statement I is false.\nGymnosperms lack xylem vessels, and vessels are a feature of angiosperms, so Statement II is true."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q113","pn":113,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Secondary growth: annual rings","q":"Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R :\nAssertion A : Late wood has fewer xylary elements with narrow vessels.\nReason R : Cambium is less active in winters\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],"c":1,"s":"In winter the cambium is less active.\nIt then forms fewer xylary elements with narrow vessels.\nThis wood is called autumn wood or late wood.\nSo both are true and R explains A."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q122","pn":122,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Xylem: endarch and exarch","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : Endarch and exarch are the terms often used for describing the position of secondary xylem in the plant body.\nStatement II : Exarch condition is the most common feature of the root system.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is correct but Statement II is false"],"c":0,"s":"Endarch and exarch describe the position of primary xylem, not secondary xylem. So Statement I is false.\nPrimary xylem has protoxylem and metaxylem. Their relative position gives the endarch or exarch arrangement.\nExarch xylem is seen in roots, so Statement II is true."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q137","pn":137,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Secondary growth bark","q":"Identify the correct statements:\nA. Lenticels are the lens-shaped openings permitting the exchange of gases.\nB. Bark formed early in the season is called hard bark.\nC. Bark is a technical term that refers to all tissues exterior to vascular cambium.\nD. Bark refers to periderm and secondary phloem.\nE. Phellogen is single-layered in thickness.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["B and C only","B, C and E only","A and D only","A, B and D only"],"c":2,"s":"A is correct: lenticels are lens-shaped openings for gas exchange.\nB is wrong: bark formed early in the season is early or soft bark; late bark is hard.\nC is wrong: bark is a non-technical term for all tissues outside the vascular cambium.\nD is correct: bark includes periderm and secondary phloem.\nE is wrong: phellogen is a couple of layers thick.\nSo only A and D are correct."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q104","pn":104,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Heartwood","q":"In old trees the greater part of secondary xylem is dark brown and resistant to insect attack due to :\n(a) secretion of secondary metabolities and their deposition in the lumen of vessels.\n(b) deposition of organic compounds like tannins and resins in the central layers of stem.\n(c) deposition of suberin and aromatic substances in the outer layer of stem.\n(d) deposition of tannins, gum, resin and aromatic substances in the peripheral layers of stem.\n(e) presence of parenchyma cells, functionally active xylem elements and essential oils.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["(c) and (d) Only","(d) and (e) Only","(b) and (d) Only","(a) and (b) Only"],"c":3,"s":"The dark brown, hard inner part of old secondary xylem is the heartwood.\nSecondary metabolites are deposited in the lumen of its vessels, statement (a).\nOrganic compounds such as tannins, resins, oils and gums are deposited in the central layers of the stem, statement (b).\nThese deposits make the wood dark, durable and resistant to microbes and insects.\nStatements (c), (d) and (e) put the deposits in the outer or peripheral layers or in living cells, so they are wrong.\nThe correct choice is (a) and (b) only."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q110","pn":110,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Vascular bundles","q":"Read the following statements about the vascular bundles :\n(a) In roots, xylem and phloem in a vascular bundle are arranged in an alternate manner along the different radii.\n(b) Conjoint closed vascular bundles do not possess cambium\n(c) In open vascular bundles, cambium is present in between xylem and phloem\n(d) The vascular bundles of dicotyledonous stem possess endarch protoxylem\n(e) In monocotyledonous root, usually there are more than six xylem bundles present\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["(b), (c), (d) and (e) Only","(a), (b), (c) and (d) Only","(a), (c), (d) and (e) Only","(a), (b) and (d) Only"],"a":"NA","s":"All five statements are correct.\nNo option lists all of (a) to (e), so no option fits.\nNote: Printed key is NA (no option matches); all statements are correct."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q144","pn":144,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Anatomy of Flowering Plants","st":"Springwood and autumnwood","q":"The anatomy of springwood shows some peculiar features. Identify the correct set of statements about springwood.\n(a) It is also called as the earlywood\n(b) In spring season cambium produces xylem elements with narrow vessels\n(c) It is lighter in colour\n(d) The springwood along with autumnwood shows alternate concentric rings forming annual rings\n(e) It has lower density\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["(a), (c), (d) and (e) Only","(a), (b) and (d) Only","(c), (d) and (e) Only","(a), (b), (d) and (e) Only"],"c":0,"s":"Springwood is also called earlywood, so (a) is correct.\nIt is lighter in colour and has lower density, so (c) and (e) are correct.\nIts vessels have wide lumens to carry more water for the larger transpiring surface in spring, so (b) is wrong.\nSpringwood and autumnwood form alternate light and dark rings called annual rings, so (d) is correct."}
